  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1220 PM CST Thu Nov 12 2026\n\nFantastic fall weather continued at midday, with satellite\nimagery showing a perfectly clear sky persisting. Surface\nobservations confirmed light and variable winds, and\ntemperatures warming well above November normals, into the \n60s once again. \n\nA weak backdoor cold front currently situated across central\nKansas with light northeast winds will backdoor its way into\nSW KS tonight through sunrise Friday. Associated weak cool\nsurface ridging will nose its way westward tonight, with near\nideal radiational cooling. Many locations will receive a light\nfreeze (30-32) for several hours early Friday, especially\nnortheast of Dodge City. Still, air mass will not be cold\nenough to support a hard/killing freeze (<28) on a widespread\nbasis. This cooler air mass will be recirculated through \nSW KS on increased southeast winds Friday, with models showing\n4-5C cooling at 850 mb. As such, cooler afternoon temperatures\nin the 50s can be expected. Full sunshine will continue east of\nthe ridge axis for the first half of Friday, but once the ridge\naxis arrives/passes, mid and high clouds will increase from west\nto east by sunset. \n\nRobust negatively tilted shortwave trough over the Great Basin \nat 6 am Saturday will quickly slide southeast and organize into\na closed midlevel cyclone near northeast New Mexico 6 pm\nSaturday. Forcing for ascent ahead of this system will spread\nwidespread light to moderate rain to areas south and west of\nDodge City Saturday afternoon. Dynamic lift and moisture\navailability have been consistently forecast by the models to\nfocus near the Oklahoma border, where the heaviest rain will\nfall late Saturday through early Sunday. WPC QPF guidance aligns\nwell with the global models, showing rainfall amounts near \n0.50 inch near Oklahoma, but sharply falling with northern\nextent, down to just trace amounts along I-70. NBM probability\nof QPF > 0.25 inch is near 90% across southern zones. All\nprecipitation in Kansas will take the form of rain, with any wet\nsnowflakes remaining in Colorado on the higher terrain. \n\nShortwave is quite progressive, so rain will end quickly Sunday\nmorning. Stratus clouds will persist all day Sunday, amid\nlingering light moist southeast upslope, and temperatures will\nmove little, in the 40s. The atmosphere will reach its coolest\npoint sunrise Monday, well down into the 30s, but cloud cover is\nexpected to hold, preventing full radiational cooling, and\npreventing killing freezes and any frost formation. \n\nLight south winds return Monday, which will begin an extended\nslow warming trend through most of next week. South winds will\nincrease in strength Tuesday and Wednesday, in response to the\nnext shortwave ejecting out of the Desert Southwest. South wind\nmagnitudes and the degree of warming appear a bit low/slow on \nthe NBM guidance. This synoptic southerly flow regime will \nensure the lack of killing freezes will continue through at \nleast November 20th. \n\nA much larger, stronger storm system is readily apparent in the\nmodel guidance for next weekend, November 21-22nd. This system\nwill be a significant cyclone, probably with pre-Thanksgiving\ntravel impacts on the plains, but it is much too early to\ndiscuss any details.",
